{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1594776539/12/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7ec998c9\" lines=\"14-28\">\n<description>\nDraw a blue folded corner-to-corner route through the lone marker's column.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid filled with one background color.\n- Exactly one non-background marker pixel; blue is absent from the input.\n\nRule:\n- Preserve the marker and repaint background pixels in its column blue from top to bottom.\n- If the marker is in the unique center column, connect that column to the top-right and bottom-left corners.\n- Otherwise, connect the column to the top-left and bottom-right corners.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker's column is the route spine; its central vs off-center placement selects which opposite corner pair gets the horizontal edge segments.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2ba387bc\" lines=\"6-21\">\n<description>\nSort scattered 4×4 squares into two columns by type: frames left, solids right.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Multiple 4×4 objects on bg.\n- Frames: colored 1px border, bg-filled 2×2 interior.\n- Solids: entirely filled with one non-bg color.\n\nRule:\n- Classify each 4×4 object as frame or solid by its interior.\n- Sort each group top-to-bottom by input row.\n- Stack frames in left column (cols 0–3), solids in right (cols 4–7).\n- Output is 8 wide; height = 4 × max(#frames, #solids), bg-padded.\n\nKey insight: Frame vs solid is determined solely by whether the 2×2 interior matches bg.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"aaef0977\" lines=\"25-41\">\n<description>\nReplace an orange canvas with concentric Manhattan-distance color rings centered on the lone marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an orange background with exactly one non-orange marker pixel.\n- The marker color is one of the colors in the fixed non-orange ring cycle.\n\nRule:\n- The marker location is distance 0 and keeps the marker color.\n- Each step of Manhattan distance away from the marker advances one position\n  through the cycle green, yellow, black, grey, red, teal, maroon, pink, blue.\n- Fill every pixel of the original grid by this distance-indexed cycle.\n\nKey insight: The output is not copied from existing objects; it is the\ndistance field around the single marker, colored by a cyclic palette that\nexcludes the orange background.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"271d71e2\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nMove bordered containers toward same-colored destination markers, charging gray cells to orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = 6 (magenta). Black-bordered (0) rectangles with gray (5) / orange (7) interiors.\n- A head sprite (9) sits adjacent to one container edge; a matching destination sprite lies along the movement axis.\n\nRule:\n- Each container translates toward its destination along the head-to-dest axis.\n- Steps = min(head-to-dest distance, number of gray interior cells).\n- Gray cells become orange from the head-facing side inward, one per step.\n\nKey insight: Gray cell count caps movement — a container stops short if it runs out of gray cells to charge.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c4d1a9ae\" lines=\"28-48\">\n<description>\nFill each vertical foreground band's background cells with the color of the next band to its right.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Each other color occupies a separate vertical band defined by that color's\n  minimum and maximum columns.\n- Within a band, all cells are either that band's color or background.\n\nRule\n- Order the bands from left to right.\n- In each band, replace background pixels with the color of the next band in\n  that order, wrapping from the rightmost band back to the leftmost.\n- Preserve all existing foreground pixels and all background pixels outside\n  the bands.\n\nKey insight\n- The column bands form a cyclic palette legend: a band's gaps are completed\n  by borrowing the color from its right-hand neighbor, not by changing the\n  band's existing pixels.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b745798f\" lines=\"18-33\">\n<description>\nExpand each 3-pixel L marker into a same-color corner L on an empty background.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is an odd square with one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground object is a monochromatic 4-connected 3-pixel L inside a\n  2x2 box.\n\nRule\n- Clear the grid to the background.\n- For each marker, map its L orientation to the corresponding outer corner\n  and draw that corner L in the marker color with arm length half the grid.\n\nKey insight\n- The small marker is a miniature corner; its orientation, not its position,\n  chooses which outer corner receives the expanded L.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6ca952ad\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nDrop every large foreground object straight down to the bottom while leaving small marker objects fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Objects of area at most three are stationary markers; larger objects are moving sprites.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each moving sprite from its original position.\n- Translate each moving sprite vertically downward until its bbox bottom reaches the grid bottom.\n- Repaint each translated sprite in its original color; small markers stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Object area separates the active falling sprites from the small fixed markers, and all active sprites follow the same vertical path.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b2bc3ffd\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nMove every object above the solid bottom stripe upward by its own pixel count.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background, exactly one full-width non-background row\nthat acts as a fixed stripe, and one or more 4-connected monochrome objects\nabove that stripe.\n\nRule\nClear each object from its original cells, then translate it straight upward\nby the number of pixels in that object, preserving its shape and color. Leave\nthe full-width stripe unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nEach object's area is also its vertical displacement; the full-width row is a\nfixed baseline, not a moving object.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e12f9a14\" lines=\"138-152\">\n<description>\nEmit diagonal beams from framed 2x2 blocks through frame gaps; beams interact on collision.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall color (e.g. green) forms 4x4 frames around 2x2 monochrome blocks.\n- Each frame has one or more gap pixels where the wall is missing.\n\nRule:\n- Each gap emits a beam of the inner block's color, traveling diagonally outward (direction set by gap position relative to the block).\n- Beams paint every cell they cross until they hit the grid edge or collide.\n- 90° collision: both beams turn 45° toward their bisector direction; the intersection cell is left blank (1-pixel gap).\n- 45° collision: beams merge into a zigzag (alternating cardinal + diagonal steps) with no gap at the meeting point.\n\nKey insight: Two distinct collision rules — 90° produces a gap and single-pixel diagonal lines; 45° produces no gap and thicker zigzag bands.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8a6d367c\" lines=\"52-67\">\n<description>\nComplete the framed area by expanding the unique upper template that contains the existing seed pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A teal background contains small colored template masks above a larger rectangular frame.\n- The frame encloses one fill color arranged as a partial block-scaled copy of one template.\n- The frame interior dimensions are an integer square scale of the matching template bbox.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the rectangular frame.\n- Identify the unique upper template whose block-scaled mask covers every existing fill pixel inside the frame.\n- Reset the frame interior to background and paint the whole scaled mask with the fill color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe lower colored pixels are seed cells that reveal which upper template should be completed inside the frame.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2a28add5\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nPaint each pink control pixel as a teal horizontal bar whose reach equals the foreground counts on that row.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color and one or more pink control pixels.\n- Each marker row has exactly one pink pixel; all other non-background pixels\n  in that row are counters.\nRule\n- Start from an all-background output grid.\n- For each pink pixel, count non-background pixels strictly to its left and\n  strictly to its right in the same row.\n- Paint teal from c - left_count through c + right_count on that row.\nKey insight\n- The foreground pixels are not copied; only their counts on each side of\n  each pink marker encode the teal bar length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3cd86f4f\" lines=\"6-18\">\n<description>\nShear the rectangular input down-left onto a black canvas.\n\nInput structure\nA rectangular grid of colored pixels with no internal empty region to infer.\n\nRule\nCreate a black output with the same height and width W + H - 1, then copy\neach input row r unchanged starting at column H - 1 - r.\n\nKey insight\nThe colors and row contents are preserved; only the row offsets change so\nthe left edge forms a descending diagonal.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ecb67b6d\" lines=\"138-155\">\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
